      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#3 · the political-count carrier; A02 copula; A13; C25; the Syn matheme comparison",
          "title": "The asymmetric count: Two replaces, Six is assigned, One stays withheld",
          "italic": "The scene requires all three numbered offices — and the mytheme is the appointed political counterfeit of the count: the office retains the power of assignment and gives the captive its result.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The same abstraction operates differently at the two ends. Number Two identifies an office through changes of bearer: access, command and institutional memory pass to the next administrator. Number Six identifies a person whom that office seeks to hold within an assigned position; the office gains continuity across replacement while the captive is denied authority over how his designation counts. The scene requires all three offices — One names the withheld source of command, Two exercises delegated authority, Six receives an answer that classifies him. A02 defines the difference",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "between identifying this participant and making a predicate exhaustive of him; A03 qualifies the demand to close the relation in one final determination — the institution's concealed information is not itself the Immutable Gap. The opening's movement from Two to Six compares with the native Syn matheme, whose 2+2²=6=4+2 retains two poles and their four ordered pairings, the count including the relation that generates it: in the Village's speech-act the office keeps the power of assignment and gives the captive its result. McGoohan and Markstein are not credited with QL notation.\n\nOccurrences: the replaceable Twos across the season (#0–#2, #3); the assigned Six (#0, #3); the withheld One (#2, #3); the title exchange (#0, #3).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
